Rem ---- the state a session starts from ---------------------------------

Rem Halifax's fold, in halifax's order, with seedvt100, seedkeys and
Rem seedbuzzer lifted out. The order is not a preference: it is the order
Rem WORDS prints its group headings in, and test.shoddy asserts the
Rem headings against an ordered list. Anything appended in future goes at
Rem the END for the same reason halifax appends — inserting by subject
Rem moves every later group in the listing.
Def SpkFresh() As RckState
    Let s0 = RckSeedBuiltin(RckNew())
    Let s1 = RckSeedMath(s0)
    Let s2 = RckSeedStats(s1)
    Let s3 = RckSeedSeq(s2)
    Let s4 = RckSeedStr(s3)
    Let s5 = RckSeedMoney(s4)
    Let s6 = RckSeedMatrix(s5)
    Let s7 = RckSeedDict(s6)
    Let s8 = RckSeedFile(s7)
    Let s9 = RckSeedClock(s8)
    Let s10 = RckSeedRandom(s9)
    Let s11 = RckSeedCsv(s10)
    Let s12 = RckSeedBool(s11)
    Let s13 = RckSeedShaker(s12)
    Let s14 = RckSeedJson(s13)
    Let s15 = RckSeedXml(s14)
    Let s16 = RckSeedHtml(s15)
    Let s17 = RckSeedSimplex(s16)
    Let s18 = RckSeedLin(s17)
    Let s19 = RckSeedEng(s18)
    Let s20 = RckSeedFin(s19)
    Let s21 = RckSeedAlg(s20)
    Let s22 = RckSeedNeural(s21)
    Let s23 = RckSeedRecio(s22)
    Let s24 = RckSeedIsam(s23)
    Let s25 = RckSeedScribbler(s24)
    Let s26 = RckSeedNet(s25)
    Let s27 = RckSeedTurtle(s26)
    Let s28 = RckSeedPlotter(s27)
    Let s29 = RckSeedHttps(s28)
    Let s30 = RckSeedRegex(s29)
    Let s31 = RckSeedSparse(s30)
    Let s32 = RckSeedMip(s31)
    Let s33 = RckSeedTerminal(s32)
    Let s34 = RckSeedGeo(s33)
    SpkShellSeed(s34)

Rem ---- the words the host owns ----------------------------------------

Def SpkShellName() As String
    "shell"

Def SpkSaveWord() As String
    "SAVE"

Def SpkLoadWord() As String
    "LOAD"

Def SpkTapeSaveWord() As String
    "TAPESAVE"

Def SpkResetWord() As String
    "RESET"

Def SpkShellWordNames() As List Of String
    { SpkSaveWord(), SpkLoadWord(), SpkTapeSaveWord(), SpkResetWord() }

Rem The three file words are RckTouch so WORDS groups them under
Rem "touches the world" — true of them whoever ends up carrying it out.
Rem RESET is RckPure, because building a fresh dictionary touches
Rem nothing.
Def SpkShellSeed(st As RckState) As RckState
    Let s0 = RckSeeding(st, SpkShellName())
    Let s1 = RckReg(s0, SpkSaveWord(), 0, RckTouch(Fn(vs) => SpkHostOnly(SpkSaveWord())), "( -- )", "Write the words you have defined where the session can read them back. The server performs it: call the save tool with a file name.")
    Let s2 = RckReg(s1, SpkLoadWord(), 0, RckTouch(Fn(vs) => SpkHostOnly(SpkLoadWord())), "( -- )", "Read a file of definitions back in; one bad line refuses the whole file. The server performs it: call the load tool with a file name.")
    Let s3 = RckReg(s2, SpkTapeSaveWord(), 0, RckTouch(Fn(vs) => SpkHostOnly(SpkTapeSaveWord())), "( -- )", "Write the tape to a file, exactly as it was shown. The server performs it: call the tape tool.")
    Let s4 = RckReg(s3, SpkResetWord(), 0, RckPure(Fn(vs) => SpkHostOnly(SpkResetWord())), "( -- )", "Start again: empty stack, no history, no tape, and only the words you started with. The server performs it: call the reset tool.")
    RckSeeding(s4, RckUserName())

Rem Not "type it as the whole line", which is halifax's answer and would
Rem be a lie here: there is no line that makes these work. The door that
Rem does work is a tool call, so that is what the refusal names.
Def SpkHostOnly(name As String) As RckWork
    RckWorkBad(name & " is the server's word, not the dictionary's: call the " & Lower(name) & " tool instead")

Rem ---- what a session opens with --------------------------------------

Rem Shown by the Mode T console shell, and carried by the server in its
Rem grounding so a client is told the same three things a person is.
Def SpkBanner() As List Of String
    { "shoddy sparky — RPN. Numbers go on the stack; words work on them.",
      "WORDS lists, HELP <word> explains, VIEW <word> shows a definition you made.",
      "A user word takes exactly one cell; bank a second with STO before and RCL inside." }

Rem ---- the startup file -------------------------------------------------

Rem Read by whatever is hosting the dictionary — the console shell on the
Rem way in, and the server once per engine, which means again after
Rem abandon and NOT after reset. The name lives here so the two cannot
Rem disagree about it.
Def SpkRcName() As String
    "sparkyrc"

Rem ---- one turn --------------------------------------------------------

Rem The prompt the console shell shows, and the tape's mark, deliberately
Rem the same character: a tape row is the prompt with what was typed after
Rem it, so a saved tape reads back as the session it was.
Def SpkPrompt() As String
    RckTapeMark()

Rem Two characters like SpkPrompt, so a definition split over three lines
Rem lines up down the page.
Def SpkMorePrompt() As String
    ". "

Rem One typed line, evaluated. RckEval is the whole door; a host that
Rem wants the three parts of an answer separately reads RckLine's own
Rem fields rather than this.
Def SpkDo(st As RckState, line As String) As RckLine
    RckEval(st, line)

Rem A transaction rendered for a screen: the lines to show. Both cases of
Rem RckLine are covered here and nowhere else, so no shell matches on an
Rem engine type. The SERVER does not use this — it keeps what was
Rem printed, what the line answered and what the stack became apart, and
Rem merging them is exactly what this word does.
Def SpkShown(r As RckLine) As List Of String
    Select Case r
        Case RckNext(st, out)
            Concat(out, RckShow(st))
        Case RckStop(st, why, out)
            Concat(Append(out, RckWhyMark() & why), RckShow(st))
        Case Else
            { RckWhyMark() & "the line did not answer" }

Def SpkAfter(st As RckState, r As RckLine) As RckState
    Select Case r
        Case RckNext(st2, out)
            st2
        Case RckStop(st2, why, out)
            st2
        Case Else
            st

Rem A refusal the host itself raises — a file that is not there, a root
Rem it cannot write — carried the way the engine's are and taped the same
Rem way, because to whoever reads it back there is no difference.
Def SpkRefused(st As RckState, line As String, why As String) As RckLine
    RckStop(RckTapedBad(st, line, { }, why), why, { })

Rem And a confirmation, for the same reason: "saved 1 word" belongs on
Rem the tape between the line that caused it and the stack that followed.
Def SpkSaid(st As RckState, line As String, said As String) As RckLine
    RckNext(RckTapedOk(st, st, line, { said }), { said })

Rem ---- one line, or the start of one -----------------------------------

Rem A definition, a list or a program that has not closed by the end of
Rem the line is unfinished rather than wrong. The joining and the
Rem deciding are both the engine's — RckJoinLines and RckWants — so a
Rem console shell, a LOAD and a tool that submits several lines cannot
Rem disagree about what one line is.
Rem
Rem THERE IS NO REFUSAL CASE, deliberately: a line nothing can finish is
Rem handed to the engine whole and the engine's own refusal is the one
Rem everyone should see. A stray }, a definition that can never close, an
Rem unterminated string — all SpkReady, and every message they produce is
Rem the message the engine produces.
Type SpkJoin = SpkReady(ReadyLine As String) | SpkMore(MoreBuf As String)

Def SpkJoined(buf As String, line As String) As SpkJoin
    SpkJoinedAs(RckJoinLines(buf, line))

Def SpkJoinedAs(joined As String) As SpkJoin
    If RckWants(joined) Then
        SpkMore(joined)
    Else
        SpkReady(joined)

Rem ---- counting, for the host's confirmations --------------------------

Def SpkUserCount(st As RckState) As Number
    Length(Filter(RckDict(st), RckIsUser))

Rem "1 word", "2 words" — the English, in one place, because it is
Rem needed for words and for rows and getting it wrong twice would be
Rem worse than getting it wrong once.
Def SpkCounted(n As Number, one As String, many As String) As String
    If n = 1 Then
        Str(n) & " " & one
    Else
        Str(n) & " " & many
